  <h1>Calculator Methodology</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by our editorial team. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>The 1RM calculator on this site uses four peer-reviewed prediction formulas applied to a submaximal set — a weight you lifted for multiple reps before reaching failure. This page documents each formula, the evidence behind it, when the outputs are reliable, and when you should be cautious about using a predicted number for programming decisions.</p>

  <h2>Step 1: What the calculator actually does</h2>
  <p>You enter a weight and the number of reps you completed stopping at or near failure. The calculator applies four separate formulas, each developed from different training populations and research designs, and returns either the average across formulas or the individual formula outputs. The result is an <em>estimate</em>, not a measurement. Your true 1RM can only be established by directly lifting it under controlled conditions.</p>

  <p>The practical value of the estimate is in programming: once you have a reliable predicted 1RM, you can calculate percentage-based training weights without having to test a true maximum every training cycle. Most intermediate and advanced programs — 5/3/1, Cube, Sheiko, Texas Method, and dozens of others — are built entirely on this workflow. You establish a training max (often set at 90% of predicted 1RM) and program every session as a percentage of that number.</p>

  <h2>Step 2: The four formulas</h2>

  <h3>Brzycki (1993)</h3>
  <p><code>1RM = weight &divide; (1.0278 &minus; 0.0278 &times; reps)</code></p>
  <p>Matt Brzycki's formula is one of the most widely cited in strength training literature and appears prominently in NSCA educational materials. It was developed from data collected on college-age athletes and has been validated across multiple subsequent studies. The formula is linear, which makes it especially accurate in the 1–10 rep range and less reliable above 10 reps, where the linear relationship between reps and relative intensity begins to break down. Brzycki tends to produce slightly more conservative estimates than Epley, particularly at higher rep counts, which makes it the formula of choice when you prefer to underestimate slightly rather than risk programming above your true capacity.</p>

  <h3>Epley (1985)</h3>
  <p><code>1RM = weight &times; (1 + reps &divide; 30)</code></p>
  <p>Boyd Epley's formula predates most of the other estimators and comes from data collected at the University of Nebraska on American football athletes. The multiplicative structure makes it slightly more generous at higher rep counts than Brzycki. The Epley formula tends to perform well in the 5–10 rep range and is among the most cited formulas in exercise science textbooks — it's the one you'll encounter most often in CSCS prep materials and general strength coaching resources. At lower rep counts (1–4) Epley and Brzycki produce nearly identical outputs; the gap widens as rep count increases.</p>

  <h3>Lombardi (1989)</h3>
  <p><code>1RM = weight &times; reps^0.1</code></p>
  <p>The Lombardi formula uses an exponential structure rather than a linear or multiplicative one. It produces slightly different outputs from Epley and Brzycki at low rep counts and tends to converge with the others in the 5–8 rep range. Lombardi tends to underestimate slightly at very low reps (1–3) and is most accurate in the moderate rep range of 4–8. The exponential curve also diverges less aggressively at high rep counts compared to the linear formulas, which is why it tends to produce lower predictions when sets extend beyond 12 reps — a property that makes it useful as a lower bound estimate.</p>

  <h3>O'Conner (1989)</h3>
  <p><code>1RM = weight &times; (1 + reps &divide; 40)</code></p>
  <p>The O'Conner formula is structurally identical to Epley but uses a divisor of 40 rather than 30, producing consistently lower estimates across all rep counts. This formula tends to be the most conservative of the four and performs best on higher-rep sets (8–12 reps) where the more aggressive Epley formula may overestimate. If you are a higher-rep athlete who achieves your best relative performances on longer sets, O'Conner will give you a more realistic programming baseline than Epley alone.</p>

  <h2>Step 3: Accuracy and expected error ranges</h2>
  <p>All four formulas perform best in the 1–10 rep range. A well-executed submaximal set in this range typically produces a predicted 1RM within ±5% of the true maximum. Beyond 10 reps, prediction error increases meaningfully:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>1–5 reps:</strong> ±3–5% error typical. Most accurate range for all formulas. A 3-rep set with good technique provides the best estimate-to-effort ratio.</li>
    <li><strong>6–10 reps:</strong> ±5–10% error. Still reliable for programming purposes. The 5-rep set is the standard programming baseline used by most percentage-based programs.</li>
    <li><strong>11–15 reps:</strong> ±10–15% error. Use cautiously. The exponential departure from the linear model increases spread between formulas, and the averaged output has a wider confidence interval.</li>
    <li><strong>15+ reps:</strong> Formulas diverge significantly and prediction error can exceed 15–20%. Do not use for precise programming at this rep range. A new set at lower reps will give a better estimate.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Averaging across the four formulas reduces single-formula outlier effects and is the approach used by default on this site. If one formula is an outlier from the others by more than 10%, that is a signal to test a lower rep range before locking in your training percentages.</p>

  <h2>Step 4: Factors that affect accuracy</h2>
  <p>Formula accuracy assumes a set taken to or very near muscular failure with good technique throughout. Several factors introduce additional error beyond the inherent formula variance:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rep velocity and technique degradation:</strong> Formulas assume consistent execution across all reps. Grinding through the final reps with significantly slowed bar speed or degraded technique produces the same rep count but a different physiological stimulus. The formula will overestimate your true 1RM when technique has significantly broken down on the final reps.</li>
    <li><strong>Lift specificity:</strong> These formulas were largely validated on compound barbell movements — squat, bench press, deadlift, overhead press. They are less validated on machine exercises, unilateral movements, and isolation exercises where load-to-bodyweight relationships and muscle group contributions differ substantially.</li>
    <li><strong>Training status:</strong> Advanced lifters have higher neural efficiency and can maintain technical consistency under load longer than beginners, making their submaximal rep estimates more reliable. Lifters in their first year of consistent training get more variable results from these formulas because their rep-to-1RM relationship hasn't stabilized yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Individual variability — rep athletes vs. strength athletes:</strong> Some athletes perform significantly better on higher-rep work relative to their true 1RM ("rep athletes"); others peak at singles and perform relatively poorly on longer sets ("strength athletes"). The formulas are calibrated for the middle of this distribution. Extreme rep athletes will find the formulas underestimate their 1RM when calculated from moderate rep sets; extreme strength athletes will find the opposite.</li>
    <li><strong>Fatigue state:</strong> A submaximal set performed after multiple heavy sets, or later in a fatigue-accumulated training week, will produce fewer reps at a given weight and thus understate your true 1RM. Run 1RM estimates on a relatively fresh effort that represents a genuine performance attempt, not a fatigued training session.</li>
    <li><strong>Rest periods:</strong> Insufficient rest before a submaximal testing set will reduce rep count and produce a low estimate. Three to five minutes of full rest before your testing set is the standard protocol.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 5: When not to use the formula output</h2>
  <p>The calculator is a programming planning tool, not a certification of your absolute strength. There are specific situations where the output should be used cautiously or disregarded entirely:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>New exercises:</strong> If you've been training a lift for fewer than three months, your rep-to-1RM relationship is still being established. Formula outputs will be less reliable because your performance is still largely technique-limited rather than strength-limited.</li>
    <li><strong>Competition attempt selection:</strong> Never select powerlifting meet openers based on a calculator output alone. Test actual near-maximal training attempts (92–95%) in the weeks before a meet and use those training performances, not calculated estimates, for attempt planning.</li>
    <li><strong>After a deload or layoff:</strong> The first session back from a deload week or a rest period is not a reliable baseline for programming. Systemic fatigue is reduced but the acute performance of that first session does not represent your true training state.</li>
    <li><strong>Bodyweight exercises:</strong> Push-ups, pull-ups, dips, and other bodyweight-resisted movements have fundamentally different load curves and scaling relationships. These formulas do not apply.</li>
    <li><strong>Very high rep sets above 20:</strong> The formulas were not designed for this rep range and produce increasingly meaningless outputs above 20 reps. High-rep endurance-style training requires different analysis tools.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 6: How to get the most accurate estimate</h2>
  <p>For the most reliable prediction from a submaximal set, follow this protocol:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose a weight you expect to complete 3–6 clean reps with before reaching true muscular failure. This rep range provides the best accuracy-to-safety tradeoff.</li>
    <li>Warm up thoroughly using the standard warm-up progression described in our <a href="/guides/how-to-test-1rm-safely">1RM testing guide</a>. Estimate quality drops substantially when the working set is your first heavy effort of the session.</li>
    <li>Perform the set with competition-quality technique throughout. If you cannot maintain your standard technique at a given rep, stop and count only the clean reps.</li>
    <li>Stop at true muscular failure or one rep before failure (RIR 0–1). Stopping well short of failure produces an inaccurate input.</li>
    <li>Enter the weight and the number of clean reps completed into the calculator.</li>
    <li>Examine the individual formula outputs. If they are tightly clustered (within 5–10 lbs of each other for most lifters), the estimate is reliable. If they diverge significantly, the estimate is less reliable and you should consider performing a lower-rep set closer to your maximum.</li>
  </ol>

